      "id": 1,
      "text": "Dunbar was laid out in 1857 and became an incorporated borough on June 26, 1883.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunbar%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was named for Colonel Thomas Dunbar, who led the 48th Regiment of Foot during the French and Indian War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dunbarhistoricalsociety.org/dunbar-s-history",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 19th and early 20th centuries, Dunbar housed five banks simultaneously, reflecting its industrial boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dunbarhistoricalsociety.org/dunbar-s-history",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 2.1‑mile Sheepskin Trail, opened in 2008, connects Dunbar to the Great Allegheny Passage and features a historic beehive coke oven.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.dunbarhistoricalsociety.org/dunbar-s-history",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Dunbar’s iron industry supplied coke to Pittsburgh’s steel mills, making it a key player in the region’s industrial revolution.",
